{
  "id": "pytest_dev_pytest_14954",
  "repo_name": "pytest-dev/pytest",
  "repo_url": "https://github.com/pytest-dev/pytest",
  "base_commit": "eadb5a625081a1c9db58552797fecf8c88b2508c",
  "pr_number": 14954,
  "issue_number": 14953,
  "issue_title": "`max_warnings` rejects the integer shown in the native TOML documentation",
  "issue_body": "\nBoth the [`max_warnings` configuration reference](https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/reference/reference.html#confval-max_warnings) and the [warnings how-to](https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/capture-warnings.html#setting-a-maximum-number-of-warnings) show an unquoted native TOML integer. The configuration reference declares the option as `int`:\n\n```toml\n[pytest]\nmax_warnings = 10\n```\n\nHowever, `max_warnings` is registered as a string configuration option, so pytest rejects this documented representation.\n\n### Minimal reproduction\n\n`pytest.toml`:\n\n```toml\n[pytest]\nmax_warnings = 0\n```\n\n`test_warning.py`:\n\n```python\nimport warnings\n\n\ndef test_warning():\n    warnings.warn(UserWarning(\"example warning\"))\n```\n\nRun:\n\n```console\npytest -qq --disable-warnings\n```\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nPytest 9.1.1 runs the test, then emits a traceback ending with:\n\n```text\nTypeError: .../pytest.toml: config option 'max_warnings' expects a string, got int: 0\n```\n\nThe process exits with code 1.\n\nAt the pinned revision, following #14791, values that fail `Config.getini()`\u2019s registered-type validation are reported as configuration errors:\n\n```text\nERROR: .../pytest.toml: config option 'max_warnings' expects a string, got int: 0\n```\n\nThe pinned revision exits with code 4. For this case, #14791 changes how the invalid value is reported, but `max_warnings` remains string-typed.\n\nThe same rejection occurs with an unquoted integer under `[tool.pytest]` in `pyproject.toml`.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nBased on the documented type and examples, the integer should be accepted and used as the warning threshold. Here, one warning exceeds the explicit threshold of zero, so pytest would exit with `MAX_WARNINGS_ERROR` (code 6).\n\nQuoting the value is a working workaround:\n\n```toml\n[pytest]\nmax_warnings = \"0\"\n```\n\nThis produces:\n\n```text\nTests pass, but maximum allowed warnings exceeded: 1 > 0\n```\n\nand exits with code 6.\n\nThe issue is not specific to zero: all unquoted native TOML integers are rejected. Zero provides a useful regression case because a fix must also distinguish the valid integer `0` from the empty value representing an unset option.\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>Verified implementation and compatibility notes</strong></summary>\n\n`max_warnings` is currently registered without an explicit type or default, giving it the implicit string type and empty-string default.\n\nIf native TOML integers should be supported, one tested direction is:\n\n```python\nparser.addini(\n    \"max_warnings\",\n    help=...,\n    type=str | int,\n    default=\"\",\n)\n```\n\nPytest tries union members in order. With `str | int`:\n\n- native TOML integers remain integers;\n- quoted TOML values remain strings;\n- values from `pytest.ini` and `-o` remain strings as they are today;\n- the unset value remains `\"\"`.\n\n`_get_max_warnings()` would also need to distinguish integer zero from the empty default while preserving `--max-warnings` precedence:\n\n```diff\n def _get_max_warnings(self) -> int | None:\n     \"\"\"Return the max_warnings threshold, from CLI or INI, or None if unset.\"\"\"\n     value = self.config.option.max_warnings\n     if value is not None:\n         return int(value)\n     ini_value = self.config.getini(\"max_warnings\")\n-    if ini_value:\n-        return int(ini_value)\n-    return None\n+    if ini_value == \"\":\n+        return None\n+    return int(ini_value)\n```\n\nA temporary prototype was tested with:\n\n- unquoted and quoted values in `pytest.toml`;\n- unquoted and quoted values under `[tool.pytest]`;\n- `pytest.ini`;\n- `-o`;\n- an unset option;\n- an explicit zero;\n- `--max-warnings` precedence.\n\nThe prototype passed the focused coverage listed above, and the existing `testing/test_warnings.py::TestMaxWarnings` tests passed unchanged (`11 passed`).\n\nThis is one possible implementation direction, not a requirement. A documentation change requiring quoted strings remains another possible resolution.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary><strong>History and related precedent</strong></summary>\n\n[#14371](https://github.com/pytest-dev/pytest/issues/14371) requested `max_warnings = N`, where `N` is an integer. #14372 added the option, described the work as INI configuration support, and tested configuration through `pytest.ini`; its documentation also declared `int` and showed an unquoted native TOML integer.\n\nThe closest implementation precedent is #14692, which fixed #14675. The numeric `truncation_limit_*` options had the same native-TOML mismatch, and the accepted fix registered them as `int | str` so native integers and existing string values both remained supported.\n\nThere are also documentation-only precedents. #13906 quoted native TOML examples for three string-registered verbosity settings. #14255 quoted numeric logging levels, which also accept named levels.\n\n</details>\n\n### Environment\n\n- pytest pinned revision `5d85267e3539968dad33937295b0a77b6c7eb7b3`\n- pytest stable `9.1.1`\n- Python `3.12.13`\n- OS Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9.8 (Plow)\n\n### AI assistance\n\nAI assistance was used collaboratively during the investigation and drafting; I directed the work, reproduced the behavior, reviewed the source and history, and personally verified the claims and test results.\n\nShould `max_warnings` accept both native TOML integers and the existing string representation, or should the documentation require quoted strings instead?",
  "merged_at": "2026-09-06T11:45:39Z",
